ease-in-out translate-x-0 translate-y-0 opacity-0"></path><path d="M20 9L19 8" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" class="transition-[transform,opacity] duration-100 ease-in-out translate-x-0 translate-y-0 opacity-0"></path><path d="M4 7L5 8" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" class="transition-[transform,opacity] duration-100 ease-in-out translate-x-0 translate-y-0 opacity-0"></path><path d="M4 9L5 8" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" class="transition-[transform,opacity] duration-100 ease-in-out translate-x-0 translate-y-0 opacity-0"></path></g></svg><div class="absolute inset-0 flex items-center justify-center opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 transition-opacity" style="transform: none;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-chevron-down text-secondary"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6"></path></svg></div></div><div class="flex-1 overflow-hidden text-secondary"><span class="truncate">Thought for 19s</span></div></div></div></div></div><div class="response-content-markdown markdown [&amp;_a:not(.not-prose)]:text-current [&amp;_a:not(.not-prose):hover]:text-primary [&amp;_a:not(.not-prose):hover]:decoration-primary [&amp;_a:not(.not-prose)]:underline [&amp;_a:not(.not-prose)]:decoration-primary/30 [&amp;_a:not(.not-prose)]:underline-offset-2 [&amp;_h2:not(.not-prose):first-child]:mt-0 [&amp;_h3:not(.not-prose):first-child]:mt-0 [&amp;_h4:not(.not-prose):first-child]:mt-0"><h3 dir="auto" class="text-xl">Abstract</h3>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">This paper explores the theme of incompleteness as a fundamental architectural principle across logic, computation, and cosmology. Beginning with GÃ¶del's incompleteness theorems, it examines how formal systems are inherently blind to their embedding supersets. Karl Fant's null convention logic (NCL) is presented as an embodiment of this incompleteness in hardware, with everyday analogues illustrating its practical implications. The discussion extends to functional programming's deferral of entropy via monads and Prigogine's dissipative structures, which export disorder to maintain local order. Finally, the Relativistic Scalar-Vector-Potential (RSVP) model reframes cosmic expansion as an entropic "falling outward," constrained by CMB dipole observations to disfavor multiverse scenarios with varying parameters. The synthesis argues that incompleteness is not a flaw but a mechanism for continuity, unifying these domains under a law of superset entropy.</p>
<h3 dir="auto" class="text-xl">Introduction</h3>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Kurt GÃ¶del's incompleteness theorems revealed a profound limit in formal systems: any sufficiently powerful framework contains truths that cannot be proven within its own bounds. This insight extends beyond mathematics, suggesting that all structured systemsâ€”logical, computational, or physicalâ€”are provisional, defined by their inability to encompass the metalevels in which they are embedded. This paper traces this theme from logic to cosmology, drawing connections between GÃ¶delian incompleteness, Karl Fant's null convention logic (NCL), Monica Anderson's critiques of Good Old-Fashioned Artificial Intelligence (GOFAI), Ilya Prigogine's entropy export in dissipative structures, functional programming paradigms, and the Relativistic Scalar-Vector-Potential (RSVP) cosmological model.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The arc proceeds as follows: Section 1 establishes incompleteness in formal categories; Section 2 embodies it in NCL with everyday analogues; Section 3 links it to deferred entropy in programming and thermodynamics; Section 4 extends it to RSVP cosmology; Section 5 applies CMB dipole constraints to rule out multiverse variability; and Section 6 synthesizes the themes. An appendix provides technical constraints on super-horizon inhomogeneities in RSVP terms.</p>
<h3 dir="auto" class="text-xl">1. GÃ¶delian Incompleteness and the Limits of Categories</h3>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Kurt GÃ¶del's incompleteness theorems (1931) demonstrate that any formal system expressive enough to encode arithmetic contains true propositions unprovable within its axioms. This is not a defect but a consequence of categoricity: defining a domainâ€”be it a set, logic, or computational modelâ€”excludes the metalevel conditions embedding it. Completeness within a category entails incompleteness relative to its superset.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">This establishes our baseline: systems of order are locally coherent yet globally incomplete, requiring mechanisms to signal or manage boundaries. As in set theory, where Russell's paradox arises from self-reference, bounded systems fold back on themselves, generating undecidables.</p>
<h3 dir="auto" class="text-xl">2. Null Convention Logic as Embodied Incompleteness</h3>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Karl Fant's <em>Computer Science Reconsidered</em> (2007) operationalizes incompleteness via null convention logic (NCL), a paradigm for delay-insensitive circuits. Unlike synchronous logic, which relies on an external clock as an oracle of truth, NCL introduces a null state alongside binary 0/1, signifying readiness, waiting, or incompletion. Circuits self-signal their status without external authority.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Everyday analogues illustrate this:</p>
<ul dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary">
<li class="break-words">A "Do Not Disturb" sign signals suspension of action.</li>
<li class="break-words">A learner's permit is a structured null: it acknowledges insufficiency while transmitting information about process and trajectory.</li>
<li class="break-words">An amber traffic light conveys immanence of change rather than a determinate command.</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">In each, the null is not absence but a meaningful state preventing premature interpretationâ€”a third truth value embodying GÃ¶delian incompleteness operationally.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Monica Anderson's critiques of GOFAI align here: symbolic AI systems, reliant on explicit rules, falter with ambiguity, exporting burden to the programmer. Sub-symbolic approaches, like neural networks, embrace partial states akin to NCL's null, internalizing incompleteness.</p>
<h3 dir="auto" class="text-xl">3. Functional Programming and Deferred Entropy</h3>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Programming echoes this: pure functional languages delay side-effects via monads, maintaining referential transparency until boundary crossing. This mirrors NCL's null: computation advances in an incomplete zone until controlled interaction.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Generalizing: ordered systems export entropy to environments. Ilya Prigogine (1977) showed dissipative structuresâ€”hurricanes, organisms, economiesâ€”sustain coherence by expelling disorder outward. Garbage collection exports memory cleanup; Uber externalizes labor costs to drivers. Incompleteness and entropy are displaced, not erased.</p>
<h3 dir="auto" class="text-xl">4. RSVP and the Cosmological Extension</h3>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The Relativistic Scalar-Vector-Potential (RSVP) model extends these to cosmology, interpreting redshift as entropic relaxationâ€”"falling outward"â€”rather than metric expansion. Three fields interact:</p>
<ul dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary">
<li class="break-words">Scalar field (<span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ï•</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8889em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">Ï•</span></span></span></span>): Entropic smoothing capacity, NCL's null analogue at cosmic scale.</li>
<li class="break-words">Vector field (<span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4444em;"></span><span class="mord mathbf" style="margin-right: 0.01597em;">v</span></span></span></span>): Matter/energy flows, local deviations.</li>
<li class="break-words">Entropy field (<span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">S</span></span></span></span>): Disorder measure, redistributed in formation.</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ï•</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8889em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">Ï•</span></span></span></span> defers relaxation, explaining void acceleration without exotic energy: voids "push outward" as <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ï•</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8889em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">Ï•</span></span></span></span> encodes spatial equilibration incompleteness.</p>
<h3 dir="auto" class="text-xl">5. Dipole Constraints and the Absence of Multiverses</h3>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">RSVP imposes limits on beyond-horizon inhomogeneity. Radical external variations would distort the CMB dipole, misaligning it from local Great Attractor flows. Observed dipole/quadrupole smallness implies isotropy/homogeneity extend one horizon length outward.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">In RSVP:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Î”</mi><msub><mi>Ï•</mi><mo>âˆ—</mo></msub><mo>â‰¡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">âˆ¥</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">âˆ‡</mi><msub><mi>Ï•</mi><mo>âˆ—</mo></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">âˆ¥</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mo>âˆ—</mo></msub><mo>â‰²</mo><mtext>few</mtext><mo>Ã—</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>âˆ’</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta \phi_* \equiv \|\nabla \phi_*\| R_* \lesssim \text{few} \times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8889em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord">Î”</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">Ï•</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1757em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mbin mtight">âˆ—</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">â€‹</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">â‰¡</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord">âˆ¥âˆ‡</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">Ï•</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1757em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mbin mtight">âˆ—</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">â€‹</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mord">âˆ¥</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.00773em;">R</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1757em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0077em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mbin mtight">âˆ—</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">â€‹</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel amsrm">â‰²</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7778em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord text"><span class="mord">few</span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">Ã—</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8641em;"></span><span class="mord">1</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord">0</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.8641em;"><span style="top: -3.113em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mtight">âˆ’</span><span class="mord mtight">5</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">This constrains scalar incompleteness: the universe does not sample parameters but is inherently bounded, disfavoring bubble multiverses with varying FRW-Î›CDM metrics.</p>
<h3 dir="auto" class="text-xl">6. Synthesis</h3>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Incompleteness is architecture, not error. GÃ¶del's arithmetic, Fant's nulls, functional monads, Prigogine's export, and RSVP's plenum reveal systems function by admitting limits. Completion is contextual; nulls ensure continuity across incommensurables.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">To drink the wine (Matthew 26:29) marks null fulfillmentâ€”one system's completion opens another's incompleteness. The cosmos is a plenum of null signals: perpetual falling outward, never complete, contained in a greater whole.</p>
<h3 dir="auto" class="text-xl">Appendix: CMB Dipole Constraints in RSVP (Entropic Redshift Form)</h3>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">A.1 Fields, normalization, and Î›CDM dictionary</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">RSVP uses:</p>
<ul dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary">
<li class="break-words">Scalar capacity <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ï•</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>Î·</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(x,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">Ï•</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">Î·</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span> (drives falling outward),</li>
<li class="break-words">Matter density <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Ï</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>Î·</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_m(x,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">Ï</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">â€‹</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">Î·</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span>,</li>
<li class="break-words">Bulk flow <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>Î·</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}(x,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathbf">u</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">Î·</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span> (peculiar velocity).</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Define the entropic redshift potential</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\boxed{;\Upsilon ;\equiv; \delta\phi ;-; \beta(\eta),\varphi_m;}</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\boxed{;\varphi_m(k,\eta);=;\frac{4\pi G,a^2(\eta),\bar\rho_m(\eta)}{k^2},\mathcal T_m(k,\eta),\delta_m(k,\eta);}</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Convention. Normalize <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Î¥</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Upsilon</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord">Î¥</span></span></span></span> so that the instantaneous Sachsâ€“Wolfe contribution at decoupling is</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\boxed{\left(\frac{\Delta T}{T}\right)<em>{\rm SW}=\tfrac{1}{3},\Upsilon</em>*.}</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">A.2 Largeâ€‘angle anisotropy in RSVP form</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">For <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="bold">n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf n}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7079em;"></span><span class="mord accent"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.7079em;"><span style="top: -3em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="mord mathbf">n</span></span><span style="top: -3.0134em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="accent-body" style="left: -0.25em;"><span class="mord">^</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>,</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\frac{\Delta T}{T}(\hat{\mathbf n})
=\underbrace{\hat{\mathbf n}!\cdot!\frac{\mathbf u_0}{c}}<em>{\text{kinematic dipole }\varepsilon</em>{\rm kin}\sim 10^{-3}}
+\underbrace{\tfrac13,\Upsilon_*(\hat{\mathbf n})}<em>{\text{entropic SW}}
+\underbrace{2!\int</em>{\eta_*}^{\eta_0}!\dot\Upsilon,d\eta}_{\text{entropic ISW}}.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\left|\left(\frac{\Delta T}{T}\right)<em>{\ell=1}^{\rm int}\right|\ \equiv\ \varepsilon</em>{\rm int}\ \lesssim\ {\rm few}\times 10^{-5}.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">A.3 Superâ€‘horizon gradient bound</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Model a beyondâ€‘horizon inhomogeneity by a nearly uniform gradient</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\Upsilon(\mathbf x,\eta)\simeq \Upsilon_0(\eta)+\mathbf G(\eta)!\cdot!\mathbf x,\qquad |\mathbf G|,R_*\ll 1,</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">D_{\rm int}\ \approx\ \tfrac13,|\mathbf G_*|,R_*\ +\ \mathcal O!\Big(!\int\dot\Upsilon,d\eta\Big).</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\boxed{\ |\nabla\Upsilon_*|,R_*\ \lesssim\ 3,\varepsilon_{\rm int}\ }\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad
\boxed{\ |\mathbf G_*|\ \lesssim\ \frac{3,\varepsilon_{\rm int}}{R_*}\ }.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">A.4 Effective potential and â€œfalling outwardâ€</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Write the effective potential controlling outward/inward acceleration as</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\boxed{\ \mathbf a_{\rm eff};=;-\nabla\Phi_{\rm eff},\qquad
\Phi_{\rm eff}\ \equiv\ \phi\ -\ \gamma(\eta),\varphi_m\ }.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\boxed{\ |\delta\phi|<em>*\ \lesssim\ \frac{\varepsilon</em>{\rm int}}{\alpha_\phi}\ =\ \varepsilon_{\rm int},\qquad
|\delta\rho_m|<em>*\ \lesssim\ \frac{\varepsilon</em>{\rm int}}{\alpha_m}\ }</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">A.5 Bulkâ€‘flow convergence (vector test)</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Define an entropyâ€‘weighted bulkâ€‘flow estimator on radius RR:</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\mathbf u_0^{\rm RSVP}(R)
:=\arg\min_{\mathbf u}\ \sum_{i:\ r_i&lt;R} w_i,\big(z_i^{\rm obs}-z_i^{\rm RSVP}(\mathbf u)\big)^2,\qquad
w_i\propto 1/\sigma_{S,i}.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\angle!\big(\mathbf u_0^{\rm RSVP}(R),\ \mathbf d_{\rm CMB}\big)\ \to\ 0,\qquad
|\mathbf u_0^{\rm RSVP}(R)|\ \to\ c,\varepsilon_{\rm kin}.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">A.6 Longâ€‘mode consistency (semanticâ€‘slicing gauge)</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Superâ€‘horizon adiabatic Î¥Î¥ modes correspond to a semanticâ€‘slicing gauge redefinition in RSVP. The leading dipole cancels; any residual is tied to the quadrupole via Î¥Ë™Î¥Ë™ at horizon entry (RSVP analogue of the Grishchukâ€“Zelâ€™dovich relation). The small observed quadrupole (âˆ¼10âˆ’5âˆ¼10âˆ’5) further tightens the bound in A.3.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">A.7 Bottom line</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Î¥Î¥ encodes both falling outward (Ï•Ï•) and inward pull (via Ï†mÏ†m) in the CMB.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The residual dipole limit enforces</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Î”\Upsilon_*\ \equiv\ |\nabla\Upsilon_*|,R_*\ \lesssim\ {\rm few}\times 10^{-5},</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Bulkâ€‘flow alignment and quadrupole smallness reinforce that homogeneity and isotropy extend at least a horizon length beyond what we can observe, disfavoring adjacent â€œbubbleâ€ domains with radically different largeâ€‘scale parameters.</p></div><section class="inline-media-container flex flex-col gap-1"></section><section class="auth-notification flex flex-col gap-1"></section></div><div class="order-first sticky hidden @md/mainview:block top-11 h-0 opacity-0 group-focus-within:opacity-100 group-hover:opacity-100 -ml-[36px] @lg/mainview:-ml-[40px] -left-[26px]"><div class="min-h-7 py-4 flex flex-col gap-0.5"><button class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 whitespace-nowrap text-sm font-medium leading-[normal] cursor-pointer focus-visible:outline-none focus-visible:ring-1 focus-visible:ring-ring disabled:cursor-not-allowed transition-colors duration-100 [&amp;_svg]:shrink-0 select-none text-fg-secondary hover:text-fg-primary hover:bg-button-ghost-hover disabled:hover:text-fg-secondary disabled:hover:bg-transparent [&amp;_svg]:hover:text-fg-primary h-10 w-10 rounded-full opacity-0 group-focus-within:opacity-100 group-hover:opacity-100 [.last-response_&amp;]:opacity-100 disabled:opacity-0 group-focus-within:disabled:opacity-60 group-hover:disabled:opacity-60 [.last-response_&amp;]:disabled:opacity-60" type="button" aria-label="Copy" data-state="closed"><span style="opacity: 1; transform: none;"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="stroke-[2] size-4"><rect x="3" y="8" width="13" height="13" rx="4" stroke="currentColor"></rect><path fill-rule="evenodd" clip-rule="evenodd" d="M13 2.00004L12.8842 2.00002C12.0666 1.99982 11.5094 1.99968 11.0246 2.09611C9.92585 2.31466 8.95982 2.88816 8.25008 3.69274C7.90896 4.07944 7.62676 4.51983 7.41722 5.00004H9.76392C10.189 4.52493 10.7628 4.18736 11.4147 4.05768C11.6802 4.00488 12.0228 4.00