</span></span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(129.6px);"><span><span></span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\usepackage</span><span>{noto}
</span></span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(151.2px);"><span><span></span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\geometry</span><span>{a4paper, margin=1in}
</span></span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(172.8px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(194.4px);"><span><span></span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\title</span><span>{Incompleteness, Null Signals, and the Cosmological Plenum}
</span></span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(216px);"><span><span></span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\author</span><span>{Anonymous}
</span></span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(237.6px);"><span><span></span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\date</span><span>{</span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\today</span><span>}
</span></span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(259.2px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(280.8px);"><span><span></span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\begin</span><span>{document}
</span></span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(302.4px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(324px);"><span><span></span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\maketitle</span><span>
</span></span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(345.6px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(367.2px);"><span><span></span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\begin</span><span>{abstract}
</span></span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(388.8px);"><span>This paper explores incompleteness as an architectural principle across domains, from logic to cosmology. Starting with GÃ¶del's theorems, it examines formal systems' blindness to supersets. Karl Fant's null convention logic (NCL) embodies this in hardware, with analogues like learner's permits and amber lights. Functional programming defers entropy via monads, mirroring Prigogine's dissipative structures. The Relativistic Scalar-Vector-Potential (RSVP) model reframes cosmic expansion as entropic ``falling outward,'' constrained by CMB dipole observations to disfavor multiverses. Synthesis unifies these under a law of superset entropy, where order exports incompleteness to embeddings.
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(410.4px);"><span><span></span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\end</span><span>{abstract}
</span></span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(432px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(453.6px);"><span><span></span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\section</span><span>{Introduction}
</span></span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(475.2px);"><span>Kurt GÃ¶del's incompleteness theorems revealed that formal systems are inherently limited, containing truths unprovable within their axioms. This insight extends to all structured systems, which are locally coherent but globally incomplete, blind to embedding supersets. This paper traces incompleteness from mathematics to cosmology, linking GÃ¶del, Fant's NCL, Anderson's GOFAI critiques, Prigogine's entropy export, functional programming, and RSVP cosmology.
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(496.8px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(518.4px);"><span>Section 1 discusses GÃ¶delian limits. Section 2 explores NCL with analogues. Section 3 connects functional programming and entropy. Section 4 introduces RSVP. Section 5 applies dipole constraints. Section 6 synthesizes. Appendix details RSVP constraints.
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(540px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(561.6px);"><span><span></span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\section</span><span>{GÃ¶delian Incompleteness and the Limits of Categories}
</span></span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(583.2px);"><span>GÃ¶del (1931) showed that any system encoding arithmetic has true but unprovable propositions. This arises from self-reference, as in Russell's paradox, where sets cannot contain themselves without contradiction (Tarski, 1956). Nagel and Newman (1958) clarify: systems fold back, generating undecidables.
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(604.8px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(626.4px);"><span>Truth outruns proof; extensions add axioms but new incompletes emerge. This pluralismâ€”no single final systemâ€”establishes: truth is superset-dependent.
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(648px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(669.6px);"><span><span></span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\section</span><span>{Null Convention Logic as Embodied Incompleteness}
</span></span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(691.2px);"><span>Fant (2005) reconsiders computation via NCL, where signals include null for incompletion. Unlike synchronous logic's external clock (Seitz, 1980), NCL self-signals.
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(712.8px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(734.4px);"><span>Analogues:
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(756px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(777.6px);"><span>- ``Do Not Disturb'' suspends action.
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(799.2px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(820.8px);"><span>- Learner's permit acknowledges partial readiness.
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(842.4px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(864px);"><span>- Amber light signals imminent change.
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(885.6px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(907.2px);"><span>Null prevents premature interpretation, embodying GÃ¶delian incompleteness.
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(928.8px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(950.4px);"><span>Anderson (2006) critiques GOFAI's rule-dependence, exporting burden to programmers. Sub-symbolic approaches embrace partial states like NCL nulls.
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(972px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(993.6px);"><span><span></span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\section</span><span>{Functional Programming and Deferred Entropy}
</span></span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(1015.2px);"><span>Functional languages delay side-effects via monads (Wadler, 1992; Turner, 1979), mirroring NCL nulls.
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(1036.8px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(1058.4px);"><span><span>Prigogine and Stengers (1984) show dissipative structures export entropy for order (Nicolis </span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\&amp;</span><span> Prigogine, 1977). Schneider and Sagan (2005) extend to life. Garbage collection exports cleanup; Uber externalizes labor.
