<ul dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary">
<li class="break-words">Prigogine, I., &amp; Stengers, I. (1984). Order Out of Chaos: Manâ€™s New Dialogue with Nature. Bantam Books.
&nbsp;&nbsp;Prigogine introduced the concept of dissipative structures â€” ordered systems that increase entropy in their environment. This maps directly onto the essayâ€™s claim: ordered systems (from computation to stars) maintain internal coherence by externalizing disorder. It generalizes GÃ¶delâ€™s logical incompleteness into thermodynamic incompleteness: order is bought by exporting entropy.</li>
<li class="break-words">Nicolis, G., &amp; Prigogine, I. (1977). Self-Organization in Nonequilibrium Systems. Wiley.
&nbsp;&nbsp;Provides the rigorous thermodynamic formulation behind dissipative structures, offering equations that explain how entropy production can drive emergent order. This is the scientific backbone for the essayâ€™s Prigogine citation â€” showing mathematically how ordered subsystems accelerate entropy but move it outward.</li>
<li class="break-words">Schneider, E. D., &amp; Sagan, D. (2005). Into the Cool: Energy Flow, Thermodynamics, and Life. University of Chicago Press.
&nbsp;&nbsp;Extends Prigogineâ€™s insights to biological systems, arguing that life itself is best understood as a dissipative structure exporting entropy. This provides a bridge to computational and cosmological analogies: whether circuits, organisms, or galaxies, each sustains order by embedding in a higher field of energy flow.</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Cosmology, Dark Energy, and Acceleration</p>
<ul dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary">
<li class="break-words">Einstein, A. (1917). Kosmologische Betrachtungen zur allgemeinen RelativitÃ¤tstheorie. Sitzungsberichte der KÃ¶niglich Preussischen Akademie der Wissenschaften, 142â€“152.
&nbsp;&nbsp;Einstein introduced the cosmological constant (Î›) to achieve a static universe. Although later abandoned, this move exemplifies the â€œhigher-order adjustmentâ€ theme: the systemâ€™s equations required embedding in a broader parameter to remain consistent.</li>
<li class="break-words">de Sitter, W. (1917). On the Relativity of Inertia: Remarks Concerning Einsteinâ€™s Latest Hypothesis. Proceedings of the Royal Netherlands Academy of Arts and Sciences, 19, 1217â€“1225.
&nbsp;&nbsp;De Sitter discovered the vacuum-dominated solution where space expands exponentially even without matter. This anticipates Whittleâ€™s â€œfalling outwardâ€ argument, showing how the system can evolve not by matter dynamics but by vacuum embedding.</li>
<li class="break-words">Peebles, P. J. E., &amp; Ratra, B. (2003). The Cosmological Constant and Dark Energy. Reviews of Modern Physics, 75, 559â€“606.
&nbsp;&nbsp;A comprehensive review of dark energy, situating Î› as vacuum energy. Provides the modern cosmological grounding for the essayâ€™s link between externalization of entropy and cosmic acceleration.</li>
<li class="break-words">Riess, A. G., et al. (1998). Observational Evidence from Supernovae for an Accelerating Universe and a Cosmological Constant. The Astronomical Journal, 116(3), 1009â€“1038.
&nbsp;&nbsp;Landmark observational paper providing empirical evidence for cosmic acceleration. Demonstrates how externalization (here, vacuum energy) manifests observationally.</li>
<li class="break-words">Perlmutter, S., et al. (1999). Measurements of Omega and Lambda from 42 High-Redshift Supernovae. The Astrophysical Journal, 517(2), 565â€“586.
&nbsp;&nbsp;Independent confirmation of acceleration. Together with Riess et al., these papers anchor the essayâ€™s cosmological arc.</li>
<li class="break-words">Dodelson, S. (2003). Modern Cosmology. Academic Press.
&nbsp;&nbsp;Textbook treatment of cosmological models. Offers mathematical structure (FRW metrics, Î›CDM parameters) that contextualizes why isotropy/homogeneity constrain external universes â€” supporting your claim that â€œthe universe doesnâ€™t try all parameters.â€</li>
<li class="break-words">Whittle, M. (2006). The Cosmic Symphony: The Universe Revealed. Cambridge University Press.
&nbsp;&nbsp;Accessible overview of modern cosmology, focusing on energy flows and cosmic history. Useful for connecting thermodynamic analogies to cosmic dynamics.</li>
<li class="break-words">Whittle, M. (2015). Cosmology (ASTR 1230, University of Virginia). Lecture 12: â€œFalling Outward.â€
&nbsp;&nbsp;Explicitly describes vacuum energy as gravitationally â€œfalling outward,â€ generating runaway expansion. This provides the keystone metaphor for the essayâ€™s cosmological section â€” vacuum as a dissipative system exporting its â€œorderâ€ into larger voids.</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Philosophical and Cognitive Parallels</p>
<ul dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary">
<li class="break-words">Anderson, M. (2006). After Phrenology: Neural Reuse and the Interactive Brain. MIT Press.
&nbsp;&nbsp;Argues against modular cognitive theories, emphasizing neural reuse. This parallels Fantâ€™s invocation logic: systems are not self-contained modules but continually invoke higher contexts.</li>
<li class="break-words">Clark, A. (2013). Whatever Next? Predictive Brains, Situated Agents, and the Future of Cognitive Science. Behavioral and Brain Sciences, 36(3), 181â€“204.
&nbsp;&nbsp;Clarkâ€™s predictive processing model shows how cognition depends on minimizing error via external feedback loops. This maps to the essayâ€™s claim that no system is complete internally; predictive brains externalize to their environment.</li>
<li class="break-words">Hohwy, J. (2013). The Predictive Mind. Oxford University Press.
&nbsp;&nbsp;Offers a detailed account of predictive coding as the organizing principle of mind. Serves as a philosophical parallel to Prigogine: cognitive systems maintain order by externalizing uncertainty, mirroring how physical systems externalize entropy.</li>
</ul>
